    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Morpheme agglutination is a morphological process where distinct, meaningful units (morphemes) are linearly chained or \"glued\" together onto a root or stem. Unlike fusion, which blends morphemes into a new form, agglutination keeps the individual morphemes recognizable, allowing each one to maintain its distinct semantic or grammatical function. This layering creates highly complex words that are transparently built, like assembly language, where each part clearly contributes a specific piece of meaning to the whole.",
      "source": "target",
